                        "entry_id": "c63e0bc4-3572-31f5-ab8d-2a046ae4bd3d",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A type of profound meditation."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "Hinduism",
                            "countable",
                            "uncountable"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "fbf139d4-7e76-4f68-830a-5e553dc683f2",
                                "sense_id": "ac80b7f6-5680-3189-b60e-16c97891d2d3",
                                "text": "Thus the Kaivalyopanisad accords equal importance to Sraddha (faith-regard), devotion (bhakti) and meditation (dhyana) for the attainment of the highest knowledge."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "a22865ec-6b21-4cfe-b5ec-4b4316f35ca0",
                                "sense_id": "ac80b7f6-5680-3189-b60e-16c97891d2d3",
                                "text": "The beginning must be made with pathawi-kasina, and then the dhyánas, &c., must be taken alternately, as from the first dhyána to the third, then to ákásánancháyatana, and so on to the end."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "fea8b642-9436-4be7-b928-747c6978aa44",
                                "sense_id": "ac80b7f6-5680-3189-b60e-16c97891d2d3",
                                "text": "Each of the three planes may be experienced through developing the dhyānas in meditation - so here we have yet another way of describing higher states of consciousness."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
